(2178527, 293453, 39007, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by kari1:</b></div><hr/><i> Snails are asexual .. they don''t need each other for this.  Nice shot. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Actually snails are hermaphrodites - all are both male and female, they mate using a "harpoon" on a cord which goes into the other snail''s side through the skin - OUCH!<br/> <br/>Both snails send the harpoons and both lay eggs. I was lucky enough to see it once in my front garden....<br/> <br/>Love the photo, it expresses the concept in a very humourous way and funnily enough better than a true life shot of snails mating would have.<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by kari1: Snails are asexual .. they don''t need each other for this.  Nice shot.   Actually snails are hermaphrodites - all are both male and female, they mate using a "harpoon" on a cord which goes into the other snail''s side through the skin - OUCH! Both snails send the harpoons and both lay eggs. I was lucky enough to see it once in my front garden.... Love the photo, it expresses the concept in a very humourous way and funnily enough better than a true life shot of snails mating would have.', '2006-02-24 04:40:44.000000', NULL, 0),
